#!/usr/bin/env autodq
# %% [markdown] Complete inventory operations analytics

This notebook demonstrates AutoDQ's complete operational analytics workflow
for a multi-echelon inventory network. It combines data-quality controls,
reusable datasets, schema and drift monitoring, operational KPIs, process and
bottleneck analysis, root-cause signals, inventory positioning, rebalancing,
visualization, dashboards, reports, exports, and session inspection.

The source is an inventory snapshot rather than an event log. Operational
process analysis therefore treats each SKU-location record as work, echelon as
the process stage, lead time as duration, and a positive backorder as an
operational exception.
# %% [markdown] 1. Load and register the inventory snapshot

Load the network snapshot and normalize its date before creating a reusable
named dataset.
# %% [Load inventory network]
DATASET '../datasets/sample/inventory_network.csv';
# %% [Normalize snapshot date]
SET TYPE "Snapshot Date" datetime FORMAT 'YYYY-MM-DD';
# %% [Register the raw inventory snapshot]
LET "Inventory Raw" = CURRENT OVERWRITE;
# %% [List registered datasets]
LIST DATASETS;
# %% [Preview inventory records]
HEAD 20;
# %% [markdown] 2. Establish data quality

Profile and diagnose the snapshot, inspect missing and duplicate records, and
run a reusable quality gate over the operationally critical fields.
# %% [Profile inventory data]
PROFILE "Inventory Raw";
# %% [Diagnose inventory quality]
DIAGNOSE "Inventory Raw";
# %% [Calculate descriptive statistics]
STATISTICS "Inventory Raw";
# %% [Inspect missing values]
MISSING SUMMARY;
# %% [Inspect exact duplicate rows]
DUPLICATES SUMMARY;
# %% [Add identity quality tests]
ASSERT SUITE ADD inventory_operations_gate SKU NOT NULL
    NAME 'Every inventory record has an SKU';
ASSERT SUITE ADD inventory_operations_gate Location NOT NULL
    NAME 'Every inventory record has a location';
# %% [Add inventory measure tests]
ASSERT SUITE ADD inventory_operations_gate "On Hand" MIN 0
    NAME 'On-hand inventory is non-negative';
ASSERT SUITE ADD inventory_operations_gate "Daily Demand" MIN 0
    NAME 'Daily demand is non-negative';
# %% [Add policy quality tests]
ASSERT SUITE ADD inventory_operations_gate "Lead Time Days" MIN 0
    NAME 'Lead time is non-negative';
ASSERT SUITE ADD inventory_operations_gate "Storage Capacity" MIN 1
    NAME 'Storage capacity is positive';
# %% [Inspect the quality suite]
ASSERT SUITE SHOW inventory_operations_gate;
# %% [Run the inventory quality gate]
ASSERT SUITE RUN inventory_operations_gate FAIL ON never;
# %% [markdown] 3. Create operational measures

Create a row-level inventory cost and a transparent exception flag. These
features make generic operational cost and outcome KPIs meaningful for this
snapshot.
# %% [Calculate inventory cost]
FEATURE CREATE "Inventory Cost" METHOD expression
    EXPRESSION '`On Hand` * `Unit Cost`';
# %% [Flag records carrying backorders]
FEATURE CREATE "Backorder Failed" METHOD flag_greater_equal
    COLUMN Backorders EXPRESSION '1';
# %% [Register the operational dataset]
LET "Operational Inventory" = ENGINEERED OVERWRITE;
# %% [Preview operational measures]
SELECT SKU,Location,Echelon,"On Hand",Backorders,
       "Inventory Cost","Backorder Failed"
FROM "Operational Inventory"
ORDER BY Backorders DESC;
# %% [markdown] 4. Govern schema and drift

Create a concise structural contract and a reusable drift baseline for future
inventory snapshots.
# %% [Infer the inventory contract]
CONTRACT inventory_operations_contract FROM "Operational Inventory"
    VERSION 1.0.0
    EXTRA COLUMNS warning
    INFER RANGES false
    INFER CATEGORIES true
    OVERWRITE;
# %% [Require inventory identities]
CONTRACT inventory_operations_contract REQUIRE SKU
    TYPE string NOT NULL SEVERITY error;
CONTRACT inventory_operations_contract REQUIRE Location
    TYPE string NOT NULL SEVERITY error;
# %% [Require valid inventory measures]
CONTRACT inventory_operations_contract REQUIRE "On Hand"
    TYPE numeric NOT NULL MIN 0 SEVERITY error;
CONTRACT inventory_operations_contract REQUIRE "Daily Demand"
    TYPE numeric NOT NULL MIN 0 SEVERITY error;
# %% [Check the inventory contract]
CHECK CONTRACT inventory_operations_contract
    ON "Operational Inventory" FAIL ON never;
# %% [Create the inventory drift baseline]
BASELINE inventory_operations_baseline
    FROM "Operational Inventory" OVERWRITE;
# %% [Check the current snapshot for drift]
CHECK DRIFT inventory_operations_baseline ON "Operational Inventory"
    CONTRACT inventory_operations_contract
    SENSITIVITY normal
    FAIL ON never;
# %% [markdown] 5. Build reusable operational summaries

Use ADQL aggregation to summarize inventory exposure by echelon and location.
# %% [Create echelon summary]
LET "Echelon Summary" = SELECT Echelon,
                             COUNT(*) AS "Sku Locations",
                             SUM("On Hand") AS "On Hand",
                             SUM("On Order") AS "On Order",
                             SUM(Backorders) AS backorders,
                             SUM("Daily Demand") AS "Daily Demand",
                             AVG("Lead Time Days") AS "Average Lead Time"
                      FROM "Operational Inventory"
                      GROUP BY Echelon
                      ORDER BY backorders DESC
                      OVERWRITE;
# %% [Display echelon summary]
SELECT * FROM "Echelon Summary"
ORDER BY backorders DESC;
# %% [Create location summary]
LET "Location Summary" = SELECT Location,Echelon,
                              COUNT(*) AS "Sku Count",
                              SUM("On Hand") AS "On Hand",
                              SUM(Backorders) AS backorders,
                              SUM("Daily Demand") AS "Daily Demand",
                              SUM("Inventory Cost") AS "Inventory Cost"
                       FROM "Operational Inventory"
                       GROUP BY Location,Echelon
                       ORDER BY backorders DESC
                       OVERWRITE;
# %% [Display location summary]
SELECT * FROM "Location Summary"
ORDER BY backorders DESC;
# %% [Inspect active backorders]
SELECT SKU,Location,Echelon,"Parent Location",
       "On Hand","On Order",Backorders,"Daily Demand","Lead Time Days"
FROM "Operational Inventory"
WHERE Backorders > 0
ORDER BY Backorders DESC;
# %% [markdown] 6. Run complete operational analytics

The explicit mappings keep every operational command consistent. The five
views cover recognition, KPIs, process segments and time trends, bottleneck
ranking, and evidence-based root-cause signals.
# %% [Recognize the inventory operation]
OPERATIONS DATASET "Operational Inventory"
    ENTITY SKU
    TIME "Snapshot Date"
    STATUS "Backorder Failed"
    STAGE Echelon
    DURATION "Lead Time Days"
    COST "Inventory Cost"
    QUANTITY "On Hand"
    CAPACITY "Storage Capacity"
    GROUP BY Location,Echelon
    SLA 5
    PERIOD day
    TOP 20;
# %% [Calculate operational KPIs]
KPI DATASET "Operational Inventory"
    ENTITY SKU
    TIME "Snapshot Date"
    STATUS "Backorder Failed"
    DURATION "Lead Time Days"
    COST "Inventory Cost"
    QUANTITY "On Hand"
    CAPACITY "Storage Capacity"
    SLA 5;
# %% [Analyze process stages and time trends]
PROCESS DATASET "Operational Inventory"
    TIME "Snapshot Date"
    STATUS "Backorder Failed"
    STAGE Echelon
    DURATION "Lead Time Days"
    GROUP BY Location,Echelon
    SLA 5
    PERIOD day
    TOP 20;
# %% [Rank operational bottlenecks]
BOTTLENECKS DATASET "Operational Inventory"
    STATUS "Backorder Failed"
    STAGE Echelon
    DURATION "Lead Time Days"
    QUANTITY "On Hand"
    CAPACITY "Storage Capacity"
    GROUP BY Location,Echelon
    SLA 5
    TOP 20;
# %% [Inspect root-cause signals]
ROOT CAUSE DATASET "Operational Inventory"
    STATUS "Backorder Failed"
    DURATION "Lead Time Days"
    COST "Inventory Cost"
    GROUP BY Location,Echelon
    TOP 20;
# %% [Analyze operational correlations]
CORRELATION DATASET "Operational Inventory" MIN ABS 0.25;
# %% [markdown] 7. Run specialized multi-echelon inventory analytics

The inventory engine calculates position, reorder points, planning targets,
coverage, stockout exposure, inventory value, capacity utilization, echelon
rollups, bounded same-SKU transfers, and residual replenishment requirements.
# %% [Recognize inventory roles and policy]
INVENTORY DATASET "Operational Inventory"
    ITEM SKU
    LOCATION Location
    ECHELON Echelon
    PARENT "Parent Location"
    TIME "Snapshot Date"
    ON HAND "On Hand"
    ON ORDER "On Order"
    BACKORDER Backorders
    DEMAND "Daily Demand"
    LEAD TIME "Lead Time Days"
    SAFETY STOCK "Safety Stock"
    UNIT COST "Unit Cost"
    CAPACITY "Storage Capacity"
    SERVICE LEVEL 95
    HORIZON 14
    TOP 20;
# %% [Review echelon and node positions]
INVENTORY DATASET "Operational Inventory" NETWORK
    SERVICE LEVEL 95 HORIZON 14 TOP 20;
# %% [Generate the rebalancing plan]
INVENTORY DATASET "Operational Inventory" REBALANCE
    SERVICE LEVEL 95 HORIZON 14 TOP 20;
# %% [markdown] 8. Visualize operational exposure

Create reusable charts for workload, exceptions, demand, stock, and numeric
relationships. They are also available to the dashboard and report builders.
# %% [Chart backorders by location]
VISUALIZE bar X Location Y Backorders STAGE current
    TITLE 'Backorders by Inventory Location'
    X LABEL 'Location'
    Y LABEL 'Backordered Units'
    THEME presentation
    COLOR '#dc2626'
    FIGSIZE '11,6'
    DPI 160
    GRID true
    LEGEND false
    APPEND true;
# %% [Chart demand against available stock]
VISUALIZE scatter X "Daily Demand" Y "On Hand" STAGE current
    TITLE 'Daily Demand and On-Hand Inventory'
    X LABEL 'Expected Daily Demand'
    Y LABEL 'On-Hand Units'
    THEME journal
    COLOR '#2563eb'
    FIGSIZE '10,6'
    DPI 180
    GRID true
    APPEND true;
# %% [Chart numeric operational relationships]
VISUALIZE correlation_heatmap STAGE current
    TITLE 'Inventory Operations Correlations'
    THEME journal
    PALETTE 'viridis'
    FIGSIZE '12,9'
    DPI 180
    GRID false
    APPEND true;
# %% [Inspect reusable charts]
GALLERY LIST;
# %% [Export the visualization gallery]
GALLERY SAVE TO '../reports/inventory_network/charts' FORMAT png;
# %% [markdown] 9. Generate operational deliverables

Generate an executive dashboard, complete HTML and JSON reports, and reusable
CSV outputs for downstream planning or orchestration.
# %% [Generate inventory operations dashboard]
DASHBOARD
    TITLE 'Multi-Echelon Inventory Operations'
    SUBTITLE 'Quality, operational KPIs, bottlenecks, stock risk, and rebalancing actions'
    THEME executive
    STAGE current
    MAX CHARTS 12
    MAX ROWS 20
    CHARTS true
    PREVIEW true
    REFRESH false
    DISPLAY false
    SAVE '../reports/inventory_network/dashboard.html'
    OVERWRITE;
# %% [Generate complete HTML report]
REPORT TO '../reports/inventory_network/report.html'
    STYLE executive
    OVERWRITE;
# %% [Generate machine-readable JSON report]
REPORT TO '../reports/inventory_network/report.json'
    STYLE executive
    OVERWRITE;
# %% [Export operational inventory]
EXPORT "Operational Inventory"
    TO '../exports/inventory-network-operational.csv' OVERWRITE;
# %% [Export echelon summary]
EXPORT "Echelon Summary"
    TO '../exports/inventory-network-echelon-summary.csv' OVERWRITE;
# %% [Export location summary]
EXPORT "Location Summary"
    TO '../exports/inventory-network-location-summary.csv' OVERWRITE;
# %% [markdown] 10. Inspect the completed session

Finish with the current project state and recent audit history.
# %% [Show session state]
SESSION;
# %% [Show recent session events]
HISTORY LIMIT 30;
